  "worked_solution": "First, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4=\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+3}{4}=\\frac{11}{4}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}\\times \\frac{11}{4}=\\frac{4\\times 11}{1\\times 4}=\\frac{44}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{44}{4}=\\frac{44÷4}{4÷4}=\\frac{11}{1}=11\n$$",
